#front[Preface]

Why should the location of a complex zero tell us how many primes there are below a given number? Riemann's 1859 paper connected the two questions. The zeta function has a product indexed by primes, and its zeros enter a formula for the fluctuations in their count. The first two chapters trace this connection from Euler through the prime number theorem, Hardy's zeros on the critical line and the arithmetic of curves, to Levent Alpöge's announcement of 20 November 2026.

Four chapters supply tools for the proof. The Fourier transform of a Gaussian gives the functional equation. A character modulo four separates primes into two progressions. An index in a rank-two lattice relates divisibility to volume. A triangular matrix leads to Galois cohomology. After Chapter 6 an interlude returns to Riemann's formula and the classical zero count. Readers familiar with these tools can begin with Part II.

Part II follows the argument Alpöge developed with Claude. A zero would force a positive fluctuation of a prime sum on every sufficiently late interval of a certain width. The arithmetic construction provides an upper bound on arbitrarily late intervals of that width. We derive the analytic criterion, the finite averages, the interval selection and the final exponent. For the deeper results on theta kernels, covolumes and Galois representations, we give the hypotheses and link to their proofs in the release. The book assumes a first graduate course in complex analysis and some elementary algebraic number theory. Worked problems and a guide to the formal statements follow the proof.

*Acknowledgments.* I thank Levent Alpöge for the construction, for publishing its formal development, and for his account of its origins. I thank Claude for its contribution to the development of the proof with Alpöge. The selection of arguments, examples, and notation in this exposition is my own.

